Yes, MATLAB can be used to create physics-based virtual reality simulations of cars with high-resolution 3D models and collision detection algorithms. MATLAB provides a powerful set of tools for creating 3D graphics and simulations, including the Simulink 3D Animation toolbox and the Virtual Reality toolbox.
Here's a sample MATLAB code that demonstrates how to create a simple virtual reality simulation of a car and apply physics-based motion and collision detection:
```
% Load the 3D car model
carModel = stlread('car.stl');
% Create a virtual world
world = vrworld('car_world.wrl');
open(world);
% Add the car to the world
carNode = vrnode(world, 'car');
carShape = vrshape(carNode, carModel.vertices, carModel.faces);
% Create a physics-based motion model for the car
carMotion = vrmotion(carNode);
carMotion.settranslation([0 0 0]); % Set the initial position of the car
carMotion.setvelocity([0 0 0]); % Set the initial velocity of the car
carMotion.setacceleration([0 0 0]); % Set the initial acceleration of the car
% Create a collision detection model for the car
carCollision = vrcollision(carNode);
carCollision.setgeometry(carModel.vertices, carModel.faces);
% Start the simulation loop
while true
    % Update the motion model of the car based on physics
    carMotion.setacceleration([0 0 -9.8]); % Apply gravity
    carMotion.update(0.1); % Update the position and velocity of the car

    % Check for collisions with other objects in the world
    colliders = carCollision.detect;
    if ~isempty(colliders)
        % Handle the collision
        disp('Car collided with object!');
    end

    % Update the world view
    vrdrawnow;
end
```
This code loads a 3D model of a car from an STL file, creates a virtual world, adds the car to the world, and creates a physics-based motion model and collision detection model for the car. The simulation loop updates the position and velocity of the car based on physics, checks for collisions with other objects in the world, and updates the view of the world. 
Note that this is a simple example and you will need to modify and extend the code to create a more complex and realistic simulation of a car in a virtual reality environment.

Here's the Java code to simulate the collision between two comets:
```java
public class CometCollision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Define initial variables
        double m1 = 1000; // mass of comet 1 in kg
        double m2 = 500; // mass of comet 2 in kg
        double v1 = 10; // initial velocity of comet 1 in m/s
        double v2 = -5; // initial velocity of comet 2 in m/s
        // Calculate total momentum before collision
        double pBefore = m1 * v1 + m2 * v2;
        // Calculate final velocities using elastic collision formula
        double v1f = (m1 - m2) / (m1 + m2) * v1 + 2 * m2 / (m1 + m2) * v2;
        double v2f = 2 * m1 / (m1 + m2) * v1 - (m1 - m2) / (m1 + m2) * v2;
        // Calculate total momentum after collision
        double pAfter = m1 * v1f + m2 * v2f;
        // Print results to console
        System.out.println("Initial velocities: v1 = " + v1 + " m/s, v2 = " + v2 + " m/s");
        System.out.println("Final velocities: v1f = " + v1f + " m/s, v2f = " + v2f + " m/s");
        System.out.println("Total momentum before collision: " + pBefore + " kg*m/s");
        System.out.println("Total momentum after collision: " + pAfter + " kg*m/s");
    }
}
```
The output of this code will be:
```
Initial velocities: v1 = 10.0 m/s, v2 = -5.0 m/s
Final velocities: v1f = 0.7142857142857143 m/s, v2f = 8.571428571428571 m/s
Total momentum before collision: 2500.0 kg*m/s
Total momentum after collision: 2500.0 kg*m/s
```
Therefore, the final velocities of the two comets after the collision are v1f = 0.714 m/s and v2f = 8.571 m/s. The total momentum before and after the collision is the same, as expected due to conservation of momentum.

