One potential challenge when merging arrays with large amounts of data is that the Union method can be computationally expensive, especially if the arrays are not sorted. In such cases, it may be more efficient to use a HashSet to keep track of the unique elements as they are being merged. Here's an example of how this can be done:
```
int[] array1 = { 1, 2, 3 };
int[] array2 = { 3, 4, 5 };
HashSet<int> mergedSet = new HashSet<int>(array1);
foreach (int element in array2)
{
    if (!mergedSet.Contains(element))
    {
        mergedSet.Add(element);
    }
}
