How can I calculate the median and mode of a given dataset using Go code? And can you explain the differences between mean, median, and mode in terms of interpreting the central tendency of the data?
Assuming the dataset is stored in a slice of integers called "data", you can calculate the median in Go using the following code:
sort.Ints(data)
mid := len(data) / 2
if len(data)%2 == 0 {
    median = (data[mid-1] + data[mid]) / 2
} else {
    median = data[mid]
}
To calculate the mode, you can use a map to keep track of the frequency of each value in the dataset:
freq := make(map[int]int)
for _, val := range data {
    freq[val]++
}
mode := 0
maxFreq := 0
for val, count := range freq {
    if count > maxFreq {
        mode = val
        maxFreq = count
    }
}
As for the differences between mean, median, and mode: 
- Mean is the average value of the dataset and is sensitive to outliers. 
- Median is the middle value of the dataset and is less sensitive to outliers. 
- Mode is the most frequent value in the dataset and is useful for identifying peaks in the data.
To calculate these measures using SQL, you can use the following queries:
-- Mean
SELECT AVG(Value) FROM dataset;
-- Median
SELECT AVG(Value)
FROM (
  SELECT Value
  FROM dataset
  ORDER BY Value
  LIMIT 2 - (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM dataset) % 2
  OFFSET (SELECT (COUNT(*) - 1) / 2 FROM dataset)
);
-- Mode
SELECT Value
FROM (
  SELECT Value, COUNT(*) as freq
  FROM dataset
  GROUP BY Value
  ORDER BY freq DESC
  LIMIT 1
);

