```
public static void main(String[] args) {
    int[] arr = {1, 2, 3};
    printPermutations(arr, 0);
}

public static void printPermutations(int[] arr, int index) {
    if (index == arr.length) {
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
        return;
    }

    for (int i = index; i < arr.length; i++) {
        swap(arr, index, i);
        printPermutations(arr, index + 1);
        swap(arr, index, i);
    }
}

public static void swap(int[] arr, int i, int j) {
    int temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[j];
    arr[j] = temp;
}
```

The above program uses a recursive function to swap elements in the array and print all possible permutations. The function `printPermutations` takes two arguments, the array `arr` and the current index `index` to swap elements with.

The base case for the recursion is when the `index` is equal to the length of the array, in which case the current permutation is printed. The for loop iterates through the array starting from the current index, and for each iteration, it swaps the element at the current index with the element at the loop index, then calls `printPermutations` with the incremented index. After returning from the recursive call, the elements are swapped back to their original positions before the next iteration of the loop.

