Sure, here's an example JavaScript function that generates a list of 10 educational podcasts for young adults based on their interests using the ListenNotes API:
```javascript
async function generatePodcastList(interests) {
  const response = await fetch(`https://listen-api.listennotes.com/api/v2/search?q=${interests}&type=podcast&language=English&safe_mode=1&sort_by=most_listened`, {
    headers: {
      'X-ListenAPI-Key': 'YOUR_API_KEY_HERE'
    }
  });
  const data = await response.json();
  const podcasts = data.results.filter(podcast => !podcast.description.includes('explicit') && !podcast.description.includes('mature'));
  const sortedPodcasts = podcasts.sort((a, b) => b.total_episodes - a.total_episodes);
  const topTenPodcasts = sortedPodcasts.slice(0, 10);
  const podcastList = topTenPodcasts.map(podcast => `${podcast.title_original} - ${podcast.publisher_original}`);
  return podcastList;
}
```
This function uses the `fetch` function to make an API request to ListenNotes, passing in the user's interests as a query parameter. It also sets headers to include the ListenNotes API key, which you would need to replace with your own API key.
