To retrieve the percentage of water compared to the total surface area of each planet in the solar system, you will need to have access to a planetary database that contains information about the surface area and water content of each planet. Once you have access to this database, you can use C++ to retrieve the necessary information and calculate the percentage of water for each planet.
Here is an example code snippet that demonstrates how to retrieve the percentage of water for each planet in the solar system:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
struct Planet {
    string name;
    double surface_area;
    double water_content;
};
int main() {
    vector<Planet> planets = {
        {"Mercury", 7.48e7, 0},
        {"Venus", 4.6e8, 0},
        {"Earth", 5.1e8, 0.71},
        {"Mars", 1.45e8, 0.01},
        {"Jupiter", 6.22e10, 0.016},
        {"Saturn", 4.27e10, 0.047},
        {"Uranus", 8.15e9, 0.011},
        {"Neptune", 7.62e9, 0.017}
    };
    for (const auto& planet : planets) {
        double water_percentage = (planet.water_content / planet.surface_area) * 100;
        cout << planet.name << " has " << water_percentage << "% water." << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code defines a `Planet` struct that contains the name, surface area, and water content of each planet. It then creates a vector of `Planet` objects representing the planets in the solar system. The `for` loop iterates over each planet in the vector, calculates the percentage of water for each planet, and outputs the result to the console.
To determine which planet has the highest percentage of water, you can modify the code to keep track of the planet with the highest percentage of water as you iterate over the vector. Here is an example code snippet that demonstrates how to do this:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
struct Planet {
    string name;
    double surface_area;
    double water_content;
};
int main() {
    vector<Planet> planets = {
        {"Mercury", 7.48e7, 0},
        {"Venus", 4.6e8, 0},
        {"Earth", 5.1e8, 0.71},
        {"Mars", 1.45e8, 0.01},
        {"Jupiter", 6.22e10, 0.016},
        {"Saturn", 4.27e10, 0.047},
        {"Uranus", 8.15e9, 0.011},
        {"Neptune", 7.62e9, 0.017}
    };
    double highest_water_percentage = 0;
    string planet_with_highest_water_percentage;
    for (const auto& planet : planets) {
        double water_percentage = (planet.water_content / planet.surface_area) * 100;
        cout << planet.name << " has " << water_percentage << "% water." << endl;
        if (water_percentage > highest_water_percentage) {
            highest_water_percentage = water_percentage;
            planet_with_highest_water_percentage = planet.name;
        }
    }
    cout << planet_with_highest_water_percentage << " has the highest percentage of water (" << highest_water_percentage << "%)." << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
This code adds two variables to keep track of the planet with the highest percentage of water and its percentage. As the `for` loop iterates over each planet, it compares the percentage of water for the current planet to the highest percentage seen so far. If the current planet has a higher percentage of water, the variables are updated to reflect the new highest percentage and the name of the planet. At the end of the loop, the code outputs the name of the planet with the highest percentage of water and its percentage to the console.

