To calculate the median and mode of a given dataset in Go code, you can use the following code:
```go
package main
import (
    "fmt"
    "sort"
)
func main() {
    data := []int{3, 2, 5, 3, 7, 1, 9, 5}

    // Calculate median
    sort.Ints(data)
    n := len(data)
    median := 0.0
    if n%2 == 0 {
        median = float64(data[n/2-1]+data[n/2]) / 2.0
    } else {
        median = float64(data[n/2])
    }
    fmt.Println("Median:", median)
    // Calculate mode
    freq := make(map[int]int)
    for _, value := range data {
        freq[value]++
    }
    mode := 0
    maxFreq := 0
    for key, value := range freq {
        if value > maxFreq {
            mode = key
            maxFreq = value
        }
    }
    fmt.Println("Mode:", mode)
}
```
Regarding the differences between mean, median, and mode, these are all measures of central tendency of a dataset, but they each have different uses in interpreting the data. 
The mean is the average value of the dataset and is useful for understanding the overall trend of the data. However, it can be sensitive to outliers, meaning that extreme values can skew the result.
The median is the middle value of the dataset when it is ordered from smallest to largest. It is useful for understanding the typical value of the dataset and is less sensitive to outliers than the mean.
The mode is the value that appears most frequently in the dataset. It is useful for understanding the most common value in the dataset and can be particularly useful for categorical data.
Here are SQL queries for calculating mean, median, and mode:
```sql
-- Mean
SELECT AVG(column_name) FROM table_name;
-- Median
SELECT AVG(column_name)
FROM (
  SELECT column_name
  FROM table_name
  ORDER BY column_name
  LIMIT 2 - (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM table_name) % 2
  OFFSET (SELECT (COUNT(*) - 1) / 2 FROM table_name)
) subquery;
-- Mode
SELECT column_name
FROM table_name
GROUP BY column_name
ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

